Here's the tweak: instead of staying transparent once scrolled, swap the class back when the scroll position returns to the top.

```html
<script>
window.addEventListener("scroll", function () {
  const nav = document.querySelector("nav");
  if (window.scrollY > 50) {
    nav.classList.add("scrolled");
  } else {
    nav.classList.remove("scrolled"); // transparent again at the top
  }
});
</script>
```

And in your CSS, make sure the two states are explicit:

```css
nav {
  background-color: transparent; /* transparent when not scrolling */
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}

nav.scrolled {
  background-color: #fff; /* opaque once scrolling */
  box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);
}
```

Two tips for this specific page: since your header text sits over the white background of the hero area, transparent-when-static means the nav links (and the phone number) should have enough contrast against whatever is behind them — if the page top is white, the links will look like they're floating, which may actually be what you want. Also, if your nav uses `position: sticky`, everything above works as-is; if it's `fixed`, add a matching `padding-top` to the body so content isn't hidden under it.

If the "shrinking" effect is still in there, you can keep both by also toggling a smaller height in the `.scrolled` rule — let me know if you want that combined version.

Ah, this clears it up — it's the electrician site's navbar. Looking at what you pasted, the likely cause is simple: your HTML has no CSS giving the navbar a background. A `<nav>` or div only looks transparent when nothing paints behind it — there's no default background color in browsers. If the scroll-toggling code I gave you earlier was added but the corresponding CSS (the `.scrolled` class with `background: #fff` or similar) wasn't, the bar stays transparent in both states.

Quick fixes:

1. Add a base style so it's opaque even before scrolling:
```css
nav {
  position: fixed;
  background-color: #1a1a2e; /* or your brand color */
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
}
nav.scrolled {
  background-color: #1a1a2e;
}
```
And make sure the JS actually toggles that class:
```js
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  document.querySelector('nav').classList.toggle('scrolled', window.scrollY > 50);
});
```
2. Also check the `<link>` to your stylesheet in the `<head>` — if the path is wrong or the file is empty, nothing applies at all, which would explain the fully transparent look along with any other unstyled layout.

